<Layout
	title="Tech for Small Business"
	description="Clear reports, a way out of subscription software, and ongoing care — honest tech help for small businesses, on hardware you own."
	pubDate={new Date()}
	heroImage="/og-default.png"
>
	<h1>Tech for small business, without the rent</h1>
	<p>
		Most small businesses end up renting their own tools back from big software companies — and the rent goes up whenever the company says so. I help you own the important pieces instead: your reports, your files, your passwords, and your customer data. (In-house we call this work <em>Digital Sovereignty</em> — "sovereignty" just means it's yours.)
	</p>

	<h2 class="service">Reports without the software bill <span class="flag">The Flagship</span></h2>
	<p>
		Know your numbers without paying Microsoft's bill. I build clear dashboards and automatic reports — daily numbers, weekly scorecards, monthly reviews — using free, open-source tools (Metabase, Grafana) that do what Power BI or Tableau do, running on hardware you own, with zero license fees. In-house: <em>Sovereignty Analytics</em>.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Why trust it? Because I've done this at scale, with receipts:</strong> 1,300+ staff-hours a year saved by a single automation. 10+ regional reports automated end-to-end. A first-of-its-kind shipping-visibility report adopted worldwide. A mobile-printing fix rolled out across every North American warehouse of a major company. Fifteen years of data and logistics work, pointed at your business.
	</p>

	<h2 class="service">A way out of the cloud</h2>
	<p>
		One fixed price, no surprises. I map everything you're paying subscription fees for, flag what's wasted or locking you in, and hand you a step-by-step plan to replace it with free tools on hardware you own — along with the <a href="/catalog">Free-Tools Catalog</a>, the same menu I use in every audit. (In-house: the <em>Cloud Exit Audit</em>.)
	</p>
	<p>
		I take two audits a month, so each one gets real attention. If there's a waitlist, you'll know that upfront — before you book.
	</p>

	<h2 class="service">Your own mini-cloud</h2>
	<p>
		Everything the cloud does — files, calendars, sharing, passwords — on a small refurbished server that lives at your place and belongs to you, set up alongside you so you're never locked out of your own system. I run my own version in public, every compromise documented. The greenest server is the one that already exists. (In-house: <em>Homestead Implementation</em>.)
	</p>

	<h2 class="service">Old devices, new jobs</h2>
	<p>
		Retired laptops and phones reborn as media servers, music machines, or safe, locked-down devices for your kids. <strong>Guided builds on your own hardware</strong> — your hands, my guidance; you keep the result and the skills to repeat it. (In-house: <em>Device Reclamation</em>.)
	</p>

	<h2 class="service">Ongoing care</h2>
	<p>
		Peace of mind, monthly: updates, backups, security checks, and — most important — everything written down in plain language your team can follow, so the business never depends on one person's memory if someone gets hit by a bus or just moves on. Spots are limited to a handful of clients, everything promised is in writing, and maintenance happens on a schedule, not in midnight heroics. (In-house: the <em>Guardian Retainer</em>.)
	</p>

	<p>
		<em>Teach, don't sell:</em> no inventory, no hardware sales, no markups, no warranties on equipment. Your device, your hands, my expertise. My demo machines exist to show what's possible — never to be sold.
	</p>
</Layout>
